WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.400
A practical C memory baseline starts with object reasoning.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.260
Think in terms of objects, not just addresses.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:14.720
Know storage duration, object lifetime, pointer validity, ownership, and cleanup responsibility before data crosses a function boundary.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.860
Respect effective type, aliasing, and alignment rules.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:30.200
Initialize data before use, define serialization formats explicitly, avoid raw structure comparisons when padding may exist, and keep lengths and capacities attached to buffers.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:39.860
Dynamic allocation needs disciplined ownership, checked allocation-size arithmetic, careful realloc handling, and cleanup paths that handle partial initialization.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.280
After free, the object's lifetime has ended, even if pointer values still exist elsewhere.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.420
Finally, avoid undefined behavior and unclear evaluation order.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:56.600
Use clear, sequenced statements, synchronize shared memory with atomics or locks, and review memory assumptions before release.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:04.100
The C memory model is demanding, but disciplined reasoning makes defensive C code more portable, maintainable, and trustworthy.
